-- title:
--   Two-branch normalisation at a supersingular node of X₀(q)
-- statement:
--   Let $q \ge 5$ be a prime, let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q$, and let $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$ be a ring homomorphism whose zero set is exactly the maximal ideal of $A$. Let $a \in k$ lie in `ssJSet q k`, i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $k$ with $j$-invariant $a$ has no nonzero point annihilated by $q$, and assume $a^{q^2} = a$, $a \ne 0$ and $a \ne 1728$. Let $f \ne 0$ be an element of `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)`, the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the full modular function field of level $1 \cdot q$ inside Laurent series. Assume that $W.\mathrm{ord}\, f = 0$ for every place $W$ of this field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (a valuation subring containing the constants, proper, with principal ideals; $\mathrm{ord}$ being minus the logarithm of the associated adic valuation) which is centred at the pair $(a, a^q)$, in the sense that some $x \in A$ with $\mathrm{red}\, x = a$ satisfies $W.\mathrm{ord}(j - x) > 0$ and some $y \in A$ with $\mathrm{red}\, y = a^q$ satisfies $W.\mathrm{ord}(j_{(q)} - y) > 0$, where $j$ is the image of `jq` and $j_{(q)}$ that of `qExpand ℚ (1 * q) jq` under `coeffEmb`. The conclusion asserts the existence of a nonzero $c \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and an integer $m$ such that the Laurent series of $c \cdot f$, and the Laurent series of the image of $(c q^m) \cdot f$ under `frickeInvolutionBar (1 * q)`, both lie in `CharPReduction.modularLocalized (1 * q) A.toSubring red` — the localisation, at the prime of coefficientwise-reduction, of the subring of Laurent series generated by the constants from $A$ together with the two $q$-expansions `jqModC` and `jqNModC` — and that `modularRedLocHom` sends both to nonzero Laurent series over $k$. No divisibility condition on $m$ is asserted.
--
--   This is the normalisation step at a supersingular crossing of the reduction of $X_0(q)$ modulo $q$: a modular function with no zero or pole at the places centred at the crossing can be rescaled by a constant so as to be a unit on one branch, and by a further power of $q$ so as to be a unit on the other branch after the Fricke involution. It is used in the subsequent analysis of the widths of, and the slope behaviour on, the supersingular annuli.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_NodeLocalized_exists_twoBranchNormalisation_qpow_of_forall_centred_ord_eq_zero.lean

